### Title
Native Windows launchers from conda-launchers.

### Why
Conda now copies signed launchers from a separate package. Fresh installations and upgrades must select the target Python architecture and preserve the signed bytes.

### Platforms
- [x] Windows
- [ ] macOS
- [ ] Linux

### Prerequisites
Windows x64 and ARM64 systems, the final conda packages, and the published conda-launchers package from the same distribution. Keep Defender and SmartScreen enabled.

### Steps
1. Install conda into a fresh prefix, run `conda init`, and activate it in cmd and PowerShell.
2. Create native and cross-target Windows environments with `--platform`, installing Python and a noarch Python package with a console entry point.
3. Run the entry points on a compatible system and compare their SHA-256 hashes with the selected files in `share/conda-launchers`.
4. Upgrade conda and conda-launchers in the same transaction, then repeat activation and entry-point checks.

### Pass criteria
Activation and entry points work with the target Python architecture. Copied launchers retain the package hashes and valid signatures. Windows security software accepts the final production files.

### Out of scope / notes
Automated tests cover missing files, absent hashes, corrupt launchers, and transaction source selection. Defaults does not provide win-32 launchers, so a request for one must fail explicitly.
